---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import SitRiseTest from '../components/SitRiseTest.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
const chapters = [{id:'movement',label:'Moving more'},{id:'positions',label:'What the floor adds'},{id:'test',label:'The famous test'},{id:'knees',label:'Knees & comfort'},{id:'myths',label:'Myth compost'}];
---
<BaseLayout title="Why live low" description="What floor living can offer, what the research actually measured, and why a sitting-rising score is not your personal expiry date.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="The evidence, with its shoes off" title="A good idea. A few asterisks." lede="We like the floor. We also like a claim that survives a closer look. Here’s what has been studied, what seems plausible, and what belongs in the compost." />
  <div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-3xl space-y-16 px-5 pb-8 sm:px-8">
    <nav class="reading-nav" aria-label="On this page">{chapters.map(c => <a href={`#${c.id}`}>{c.label} ↓</a>)}</nav>
    <section id="movement" class="space-y-5">
      <span class="evidence-label">Tested · activity breaks</span>
      <h2 class="display-3">The useful bit: interrupting the stillness.</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">In a small randomized crossover trial, 19 adults aged 45–65 with overweight or obesity had lower after-meal glucose and insulin responses when they interrupted sitting with two minutes of walking every 20 minutes. The experiment measured short-term responses; it did not test floor sitting or establish a universal break schedule.</p>
      <p class="source-note"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22374636/">Dunstan et al., 2012 · randomized crossover trial.</a></p>
      <p class="text-lg">Our practical take: use a change of seat as a cue to move, when you can. A floor cushion can be a reminder, but a chair, a timer, or a kettle can do that job too. Trading eight motionless hours on a chair for eight motionless hours on the floor misses the interesting part.</p>
      <Callout variant="evidence" title="The bigger picture still matters">WHO recommends regular aerobic activity, muscle strengthening on at least two days a week, and limiting sedentary time. For adults able to do it, the aerobic target is 150–300 minutes of moderate activity weekly, or an equivalent amount of vigorous activity. Floor time is an optional extra, not a replacement. <a href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240015128">WHO physical activity guidelines, 2020.</a></Callout>
    </section>
    <section id="positions" class="space-y-5">
      <span class="evidence-label">Promising · mechanism and observation</span>
      <h2 class="display-3">Different seats ask different things of you.</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">Cross-legged sitting, kneeling, and squatting put the legs in different positions. Transitions also give you a chance to practise coordinating a useful everyday task. That is a sensible reason to explore; it is not proof that everyone needs a deep squat.</p>
      <p class="text-lg">A study with Hadza adults found more lower-leg muscle activity in squatting than in chair sitting. The other ground-sitting positions they measured did not show the same increase. The study helps explain why the particular position matters; it cannot tell us that adopting floor living prevents disease.</p>
      <p class="source-note"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7132251/">Raichlen et al., 2020 · observation and muscle-activity measurements.</a></p>
      <p class="text-lg">Habit, anatomy, injuries, and personal preference all help determine what feels available. There is no universal ankle-angle entrance exam. Raise your seat, support your knees, bend your legs, or choose another position. The floor is not keeping a leaderboard.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="test" class="space-y-5">
      <span class="evidence-label">Association · not a treatment trial</span>
      <h2 class="display-3">Your rug cannot predict your future.</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">The Sitting–Rising Test scores the supports and balance adjustments someone uses to sit on the floor and get back up. A cohort of 2,002 adults aged 51–80 found that lower scores were associated with higher all-cause mortality over follow-up. The test reflects several capacities at once; it did not measure the effects of starting a floor-sitting habit.</p>
      <p class="source-note"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23242910/">de Brito et al., published online 2012 / journal issue 2014.</a></p>
      <p class="text-lg">A later cohort of 4,282 adults aged 46–75 also found an association between lower scores and natural and cardiovascular mortality. Researchers adjusted for several measured factors, but adjustment does not turn an observational study into an experiment. Neither study gives an individual reader an expiry date.</p>
      <p class="source-note"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40569873/">Araújo et al., online 2025 / journal issue 2026.</a></p>
      <Callout variant="caution" title="Use the support. Keep the dignity.">Using your hands is a strategy, not a character flaw. Pain, balance difficulties, disability, body proportions, and previous surgery can change how you get up. A clinician can help you practise a suitable transfer; removing support to chase a score may make the task less safe.</Callout>
      <p><a href={href('/journal/the-sitting-rising-test')} class="field-link">Read the fuller story <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
      <SitRiseTest />
    </section>
    <section id="knees" class="space-y-5">
      <span class="evidence-label">Context matters · exposure is not destiny</span>
      <h2 class="display-3">Your knees get a vote.</h2>
      <p class="text-lg">The Beijing Osteoarthritis Study found an association between recalled time spent squatting earlier in life and knee osteoarthritis in older adults. This observational result about prolonged exposure cannot tell us whether a brief, supported squat is harmful or beneficial for a particular person. It also does not show that floor sitting protects the hips.</p>
      <p class="source-note"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15077301/">Zhang et al., 2004 · observational study.</a></p>
      <p class="text-lg">Our approach is deliberately unheroic: brief visits, comfortable ranges, and support you can trust. Stop or change position for pain, tingling, or numbness. If symptoms persist, recur, or include weakness, get assessed instead of trying to stretch through them.</p>
      <Callout variant="caution" title="Choose your starting height">If you have a recent operation, joint replacement, significant pain, dizziness, or uncertain balance, ask your care team about suitable positions and transfers. Restrictions depend on your procedure and circumstances. A chair-height practice can be the whole practice.</Callout>
    </section>
    <section id="myths" class="floor-faq">
      <p class="eyebrow">The myth compost</p><h2 class="display-3 mt-3 mb-7">Good intentions. Overgrown claims.</h2>
      <details><summary>“Floor sitting fixes your posture.”</summary><p>There is no single shape to maintain all day. Comfort, support, task, and movement options matter. Back pain is more complex than the position of your pelvis; persistent pain deserves individual care.</p></details>
      <details><summary>“No backrest means a strength workout.”</summary><p>Muscles being active is not the same as a demonstrated training effect. We have not established that sitting on the floor alone builds meaningful strength. Keep some actual strengthening in your week, adapted to your abilities.</p></details>
      <details><summary>“The floor releases your fascia.”</summary><p>A position can feel relaxing without proving a particular tissue mechanism. Evidence about foam rolling cannot simply be transferred to sitting on a rug. It is fine to enjoy the feeling without giving it a laboratory coat.</p></details>
      <details><summary>“Natural means right for everyone.”</summary><p>Floor traditions are rich and varied; bodies are, too. A chair, a wheelchair, a supported seat, and a cushion can all belong in a welcoming room. You can like low living without turning it into a rule for other people.</p></details>
      <p class="mt-6"><a href={href('/journal/what-floor-living-cant-do')} class="field-link">A closer look at the claims <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
    </section>
    <section class="rounded-soft bg-ink px-6 py-10 text-paper sm:px-10">
      <p class="eyebrow text-straw">A practical next move</p>
      <p class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl leading-snug">Make a little room for movement.<br />Leave room for being human.</p>
      <a href={href('/start')} class="mt-6 inline-flex rounded-pill bg-paper px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-ink no-underline">Find a comfortable beginning ↗</a>
    </section>
    <p class="text-sm text-inkmuted">Evidence reviewed October 10, 2026. Educational editorial content; not an individual assessment. See the <a href={href('/resources#research')}>research library</a> for more reading.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
